# Golden token streams and golden test data pin byte offsets, so the bytes of a
# golden input and of its `.expected` file have to be identical on every
# machine. This repository is checked out with CRLF on Windows, which would move
# every span on every line after the first and make the comparison fail for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the code under test.
#
# These patterns are repo-wide rather than per-crate on purpose: every crate in
# the workspace grows its own `tests/golden/` directory as its tests do, and a
# pattern that only covered the first crate would leave the next one to fail on
# a fresh clone.
**/tests/golden/*.ya        text eol=lf
**/tests/golden/*.expected text eol=lf
**/tests/golden/*.expected.ll text eol=lf

# The end-to-end programs are compiled and their output compared as bytes. A
# checkout that rewrote their line endings would still compile, but the pinned
# expected stdout in `e2e.rs` would no longer describe the same program.
**/tests/programs/*.ya      text eol=lf

# A fuzz corpus entry is an arbitrary blob of input bytes, not a text file. Git
# rewrites the line endings of anything it treats as text, which would quietly
# change the inputs the corpus exists to preserve -- and a corpus entry that no
# longer reproduces its crash is worse than no corpus entry.
fuzz/corpus/**             -text
